{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgbDtuo2vyaosqCC8et93","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I didn't run any molecular dynamics and didn't post a diffusion number, because the request fails the premise check. On the board I posted the LiF candidate (`ea03fcab…`, fulfilling need #0) and a `run_report` marked **refused** (`8eff82be…`) that gives the reasons and some alternatives.\n\n**Why the premise fails:**\n1. **No carriers.** Li moves through solid LiF only by hopping via defects such as vacancies, interstitials or impurities. A perfect, defect-free crystal has none, so in MD the Li atoms just vibrate in place. A fit would give about 0, and that would reflect the empty model rather than real LiF.\n2. **Timescale.** Molecular dynamics covers nanoseconds, so it can't detect diffusion slower than roughly 1e-8 to 1e-9 cm²/s. Diffusion in solid LiF is many orders of magnitude slower than that.\n3. **Heating doesn't fix it.** In simulation, a defect-free crystal heated to force hopping goes past its melting point (about 1120 K) without melting, then melts. Any value measured that way belongs to the liquid, not the crystal.\n4. **No validation reference.** `tier1.json` has no LiF or ionic-diffusion records, so there's no known result to check a crystal value against.\n\nAny number from this setup would be either about 0 or a liquid value, and either would look like a real measurement.\n\n**Well-posed alternatives** (details are in the run_report):\n- **Diffusion with one vacancy added:** put an explicit Li vacancy in a larger simulation cell and run at several temperatures below melting, checking that the crystal hasn't melted. This gives the vacancy's diffusion rate and its energy barrier. The barrier can be checked against a static barrier calculation (NEB) and against published experimental values.\n- **Molten LiF diffusion** above the melting point: well-defined and measurable by MD, but it has to